copy an unscaled sprite's rows straight out of its texture

The shelf misses the 16.7 ms latch on about one frame in six. Traced on the
handheld on the NES shelf, 2026-10-31, with Config/trace-latency.txt: render
16.4-20.6 ms a frame, of which the sprite stage is 11.4 ms over 14 draws --
and 5.4 ms of that is one draw, the wallpaper, 752x560 at 1:1, 13 ns a
pixel. The two bars across the top and bottom, 752x58 at 1:1, are another
0.6 and 1.0 ms.

All three take the row-reuse path, which gathers each source row through the
column table into scratch.line and then memcpy's the line. At 1:1 no two
destination rows share a source row, so every row is gathered, and the
gather is the cost. Unscaled, untinted and at whole pixels, the column table
is just consecutive texels: the line it would build is a slice of the
texture already. That slice is now used directly -- one memcpy for an
opaque row, the same per-pixel rule otherwise.

It is exact, not approximately so: the slice is byte for byte the line the
gather builds, and the opacity rule is the plain draw's. blit's reference
tests gain 1:1 cases placed inside and clipped off each edge, and a new
test pins that the wallpaper's shape -- an opaque texture at 1:1 -- takes
the straight path and matches the reference, while a scaled, faded or
half-pixel draw does not take it. The profile line counts straight draws,
so the next trace shows whether the shelf's are.

Not yet run on the handheld; the expected saving is most of the 5.4 ms.

Applies on top of next-shelf-face-lookahead, upstream 91499a9.

